-- §19 The same shape in two graphs, and the opposite verdict on it.
# One row per graph: how often a parent reaches the same child twice, and what this repository
# rules about it there.
#
# What the repository says about itself, as a query
#   A parent composing a child twice is ordinary; a fusion reaching a layer twice is a violation.
#   Both are a name arriving twice under one total, and the difference is that a query reads the
#   same relation the same way however often it is named, while a supply is counted once: the
#   first is measured, the repeated relation read once, and `checks/jagged_layer` rules on the
#   second because one total closes over both occurrences of the same supply.
#
# Both sides have to be relations
#   Otherwise this is not a comparison at all. A claim whose two halves live in a Rust map and a
#   SQL table is printed by a program that holds one of them and cannot join the other, which is
#   the shape of every defect this repository's laws refuse.
#
# Compare the verdicts, not the counts
#   The counts do not compare and must not be compared. The compose graph has far more templates
#   than the corpus has fusions, so a ratio here would be a fact about how much SQL somebody wrote.
#   What compares is the verdict column: the same structure, the opposite ruling, and neither
#   number is evidence about the other.
-- rank/compose_edges.sqlc beside composition/parts.sqlc, the same duplication in two graphs.
SELECT 'the compose graph'                             AS "graph!",
       'a parent splicing one child twice'             AS "duplication!",
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE e.splices > 1)           AS "duplicated!",
       count(*)                                        AS "edges!",
       'ordinary: a relation read twice gives the same rows, so it is read once'
                                                       AS "verdict!"
FROM (
    :compose(rank/compose_edges.sqlc)
) e
UNION ALL
SELECT 'the part graph',
       'one fusion reaching one layer twice',
       (SELECT count(*) FROM ( :compose(composition/jagged_layers.sqlc) ) j),
       (SELECT count(*) FROM ( :compose(composition/parts.sqlc) ) p),
       'a violation: supply is conserved, so one total closes over both occurrences'
